# f2 = twice. The second `item.note` of a turn is answered by what the walk remembers of the first. Where proofs are checked at run time (testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime, which --aot-harden=proof-checks stands for) such a read is walked
# once more and held against memory, BUT ONLY WHERE THE OPERATION HAS BUILT NOTHING BUT CONSTANTS. The guard that the read leans on is asked again in front of it
# (under-the-option-a-guard-that-a-read-leans-on-is-asked-again-in-the-turn.ir), and that builds two records: the read no longer looked as if it had built nothing, and went unasked: one net hid what its neighbor was to see.
# What the guard asked again builds for itself is not counted as the operation's. Two versions of the loop, two reads each, the second one read again: four reads; without that, two. Ten tests of guards; without the guards asked
# again, four.
# RUN(HELD): walk f2 mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# HELD-COUNT-4: = GetSlotOrUndefined
# RUN(ASKED): walk f2 mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# ASKED-COUNT-10: = AreMembersUnchanged
# RUN(RIGHT): run-in-shell testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# RIGHT: 20 20 20 above
# RIGHT-NEXT: exited with 0
